Close the -ENOTDIR trailing-component existence oracle.

  stat(p "/zzz")

returns -ENOTDIR when p is a hidden regular file, where an absent p gives
-ENOENT. Measured on OP15 (Android 16, 6.12.23) against all 24 blocked uids with
a 7020-path genuinely-absent control and zero false positives.

WHY THE ENGINE CANNOT ANSWER IT

link_path_walk() tests d_can_lookup(nd->path.dentry) -- a pure dentry-type test
-- on the component it has just stepped into, and bails before the next
may_lookup(). The engine's hijacked ops for that inode are never reached, so it
cannot turn the answer into -ENOENT. Verified against v6.12 fs/namei.c.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

Inside the existing `if (unlikely(!d_can_lookup(nd->path.dentry)))` block,
after the LOOKUP_RCU unlazy step and immediately before `return -ENOTDIR;`.

Two properties make this the only acceptable spot in this function:

 1. It is COLD despite living in the hottest function in the VFS. The enclosing
    branch is already marked unlikely() and is only entered on a walk that is
    about to fail; the common case never evaluates the guard. Nothing is added
    to the per-component fast path.

 2. The path is STABLE there. ghost_hidden_path() renders d_path(), which is not
    safe during an RCU walk. The stock code immediately above already calls
    try_to_unlazy()/unlazy_walk() and returns -ECHILD if it fails, so by the
    time the guard runs the walk is guaranteed to be in ref-walk mode with a
    reference-counted nd->path. Placing the guard ABOVE that unlazy step would
    read an unstable path under RCU. Do not move it up.

Returning -ENOENT from here is the same kind of return the stock -ENOTDIR is:
link_path_walk() returns the error to path_lookupat(), which calls
terminate_walk() on the way out. No extra cleanup is needed, and none is added.

RISK NOTE, AND WHY THIS ONE RANKS LAST

Of the four guards this is the one in genuinely dangerous code. The hunk is
tiny and its context is unambiguous, but a misapplied hunk in link_path_walk()
is a bootloop, not a failed boot animation. It is also the second-weakest of the
four by information content (one bit, no path, no label). If only some of the
_ghost patches are to be shipped, this is the one to drop -- see README.md.

APPLIES TO

Vanilla v5.10 ONLY, whose link_path_walk() still calls unlazy_walk(nd) rather
than try_to_unlazy(nd) and whose preceding context differs. Verified at fuzz 0
there. No tree in the builder fleet resolves to this shape -- OnePlus's own
android12-5.10 tree has the try_to_unlazy() form and is covered by
ghost_notdir.patch. Try ghost_notdir.patch first.

v4.9/v4.14/v4.19/v5.4 have two further shapes again (unlazy_walk(nd, NULL, 0)
on 4.9) and are NOT covered: nothing in the fleet is older than 5.10, and an
unverifiable hunk in link_path_walk() is not worth shipping.

--- a/fs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/namei.c
@@ -2192,10 +2192,20 @@
 			continue;
 		}
 		if (unlikely(!d_can_lookup(nd->path.dentry))) {
+			extern bool ghost_hidden_path(const struct path *gp);
+
 			if (nd->flags & LOOKUP_RCU) {
 				if (unlazy_walk(nd))
 					return -ECHILD;
 			}
+			/*
+			 * A non-final component that is not a
+			 * directory gives -ENOTDIR, which proves
+			 * the name exists. For a path hidden from
+			 * this uid, say what an absent name says.
+			 */
+			if (ghost_hidden_path(&nd->path))
+				return -ENOENT;
 			return -ENOTDIR;
 		}
 	}
